\section{Results}

\subsection{Burst Intensity Sweep}

We performed a parameter sweep over the gamma-ray burst amplitude factor
$b \in \{0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0, 10.0\}$ using the PUE-X engine. For each value
of $b$, we recorded the total number of fusion spike events, the final
ledger length, and the maximum node state norm across all reality nodes.

The sweep revealed a transition from a low-activity regime (few or no
events, short ledger chains) to a coherent activation regime in which
fusion spike events and ledger entries cluster around the burst window
$t \approx 2.5\,\mathrm{s}$, followed by a saturation regime at high
burst amplitudes where node norms approach their stability limits.

\subsection{Topological Sensitivity and Invariance}

To test spatial robustness, we randomized the initial positions of the
$^{195}\mathrm{Pt}$ and $^{238}\mathrm{U}$ nodes over the $32 \times 32$
geometry grid for multiple configurations at fixed burst amplitude
$b = 2.0$. Across all configurations, the engine produced comparable
event counts and ledger lengths, with event timestamps remaining
clustered around the telemetry burst window. This indicates that
PUE-X exhibits topological invariance with respect to node placement,
while preserving causal alignment with astrophysical input.